4 275.968 222.72 222.72 0 0 1 75.818666 162.346667v5.717333A131.328 131.328 0 0 0 501.077333 938.666667h22.016a131.328 131.328 0 0 0 131.2-131.2v-19.498667a189.269333 189.269333 0 0 1 68.693334-142.250667 319.744 319.744 0 0 0 2.218666-479.146666zM523.093333 867.584h-22.016a60.16 60.16 0 0 1-60.117333-60.074667l-0.256-11.008h142.506667v11.008a60.16 60.16 0 0 1-60.117334 60.074667z m152.917334-275.2A272.64 272.64 0 0 0 591.957333 725.333333h-44.330666v-255.445333a106.666667 106.666667 0 0 0 71.125333-100.096 35.584 35.584 0 1 0-71.125333 0 35.584 35.584 0 0 1-71.082667 0 35.584 35.584 0 1 0-71.125333 0 106.666667 106.666667 0 0 0 71.125333 100.096V725.333333h-47.018667a307.029333 307.029333 0 0 0-87.04-137.984 248.874667 248.874667 0 0 1 169.941334-430.933333 246.101333 246.101333 0 0 1 165.376 63.232 248.661333 248.661333 0 0 1-1.706667 372.693333h-0.085333z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-bc9c95fe="" class="toolcall-title-name-text">Build Skill Zoo for Transformer-Driven Robotics Policy Composition</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-bc9c95fe="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-bc9c95fe="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div> <div data-v-9445e104="" data-v-56fb9f7a="" class="toolcall-content"><div data-v-9445e104="" class="resize-container" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-9445e104="" class="slot-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-56fb9f7a="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user is providing additional context about their repository. They mention files named: annotate, compose, validate, and demo. This, combined with the build_zoo.py file I saw earlier, gives me a much clearer picture.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me reconsider what this project is:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> - Compiles primitives into a zoo gallery</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">annotate</code> - Likely annotates motion data or skills</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">compose</code> - Likely composes skills together</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate</code> - Validates skills or motions</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">demo</code> - Demonstrates/runs the skills</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This actually DOES sound like it could be related to robotics or embodied AI! Specifically:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Skill primitives</strong> for robotics (motion primitives, behavior primitives)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">A <strong>zoo</strong> of reusable skills</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Annotation</strong> tool for labeling demonstrations or motions</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Composition</strong> of skills (sequencing, blending)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Validation</strong> of skill specifications</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Demo</strong> execution/visualization</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This pattern matches:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Robot skill libraries (like Behavior Cloning, Learning from Demonstration)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Foundation models for robotics (RT-X, Open X-Embodiment, etc.)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Natural language to skill/motion systems</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Code-as-policy or language model programs for robotics</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The "zoo" would be a gallery of available skills/primitives that the transformer or policy can compose or select from. The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> generates the catalog/index of these skills.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">So I should acknowledge that I was wrong to dismiss the robotics angle, and that with this additional context (annotate, compose, validate, demo), it absolutely makes sense as part of a natural language to robotics motion system. The "primitives" are likely motion primitives or behavioral primitives, and the "zoo" is the compiled skill library that the transformer draws from or outputs.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should be honest about my updated assessment.</div></div></div></div></div> </div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----><!----> <div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">With that context, I was wrong to dismiss the robotics angle. Those filenames change everything.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">annotate</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">compose</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">demo</code> alongside <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> strongly suggests this is exactly what you described:</strong> a natural-language-to-robotics-motion system, likely built around <strong>composable skill primitives</strong>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Here's how the pieces fit together now:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">annotate</code></strong> — Labels human demonstrations or motion segments with natural language, producing the training data for your transformer</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">compose</code></strong> — Sequences or blends skill primitives into full behaviors (the "program" or policy the NL query compiles to)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate</code></strong> — Checks that a composed skill chain is kinematically feasible, collision-free, or within joint limits</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">demo</code></strong> — Executes or visualizes the resulting motion in simulation or on hardware</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code></strong> — Compiles the master library of available primitives into a browsable/queriable catalog that the NL→motion transformer can reference or sample from</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The "zoo" is likely the <strong>skill registry</strong> — the transformer doesn't just output raw joint angles; it outputs references to composed primitives from this catalog. The HTML gallery might be for human operators to browse what the robot can do, or for the model itself to retrieve from.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">So yes, this file absolutely belongs in that repo. It's the <strong>build system for the skill primitive library</strong> that the transformer composes over. I should have asked about the sibling files before concluding it was just a design system.</div></div></div> <!----> <!----> <!----></div>  <!----> <!----> <!----> <!---->   <!----> <div data-v-b1950b46="" class="okc-cards-container"><!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-b1bd13bb="" data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-assistant-actions"><div data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="segment-assistant-actions-content"> <!----> <!----> <!----><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="icon-button" style="width: 28px; height: 28px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <!----></div><!----> <!----><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="icon-button" style="width: 28px; height: 28px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" cla