98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files</code> is an integer? <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files: 1</code>? Then <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">spec.get("files", [])</code> returns <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">1</code>. Iterating over <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">1</code> raises <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">TypeError: 'int' object is not iterable</code>. Not our error.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">What if <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files</code> is a string like <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">"grasp.py"</code>? Then iterating gives characters. Each character is a string. The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">str</code> branch handles it. No TypeError.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">OK, I truly think the file on the runner must have a different structure than what I was shown. But I can still fix it by making the code bulletproof.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me write a version that:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Normalizes <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files</code> to a list at the start</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Has explicit guards for each type</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Won't crash with TypeError</div></li></ol><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment