        <span class="token punctuation">}</span></code></pre></div></div> <div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, the user might still want <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> to work. <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> currently validates <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files</code> in <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">spec.yaml</code>. The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">base</code> primitive has <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files: [{src: base.py}]</code>. The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grasp</code> primitive will have <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">files: [{src: grasp.py}]</code>. So the base class doesn't need to know about files.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">get_metadata()</code> is useful for auto-generating spec.yaml or for the manifest.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Should I keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code>? Yes, for <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">| None</code> syntax.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Should I keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">np.ndarray</code> type hints? Yes, since the project uses numpy.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">What about <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">__all__</code> in <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">base/__init__.py</code>? It should export <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Primitive</code>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Now, one more consideration: the user's <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Grasp.validate</code> checks <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">segment.get("type") == self.name</code>. The base class default does exactly that. So <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Grasp</code> could actually skip overriding <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate</code> if it doesn't add anything. But the user did override it, probably for explicitness or future extension. That's fine.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user's <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Grasp.describe</code> returns <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">"grasp the object firmly"</code>. The base default returns <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">"perform grasp"</code>. So <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Grasp</code> needs to override <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">describe</code>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">So the base class should have:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">detect</code> as abstract</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate</code> with a sensible default</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">describe</code> with a generic default</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">annotate</code> as a concrete pipeline method</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">get_metadata</code> for tooling integration</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">No <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">RobotState</code>, no <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Trajectory</code>, no <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">execute</code>, no <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">validate_state</code>, no <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">parameters_schema</code>, no <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">__init__</code> with parameter validation. Clean and focused.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, but should I keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">parameters_schema</code> just in case? The user said "we are building an annotation engine." I should respect that and not add speculative execution features. If they need parameters later, they can add a <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Config</code> dataclass or similar. For now, keep it lean.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the user's <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">Grasp</code> code again, it doesn't call <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">super().__init__()</code> and doesn't use any instance variables. It's purely class-based with <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">name = "grasp"</code> and methods that use <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">self.name</code>. This confirms the base class should be minimal.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me also consider: should <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">detect</code> accept a single unified <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">trajectory</code> dict instead of three separate arrays? Like:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">Python</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-efb858b9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="syntax-highlighter d