-71.082667 0 35.584 35.584 0 1 0-71.125333 0 106.666667 106.666667 0 0 0 71.125333 100.096V725.333333h-47.018667a307.029333 307.029333 0 0 0-87.04-137.984 248.874667 248.874667 0 0 1 169.941334-430.933333 246.101333 246.101333 0 0 1 165.376 63.232 248.661333 248.661333 0 0 1-1.706667 372.693333h-0.085333z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <span data-v-bc9c95fe="" class="toolcall-title-name-text">Evaluating a Composable Primitive Registry for Robotics Skill Libraries</span></div> <!----> <div data-v-bc9c95fe="" class="toolcall-title-container-extra"> <svg data-v-bc9c95fe="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-container-extra-arrow" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Right_b"><path d="M681.00096 549.23264a36.864 36.864 0 0 0-0.08192-52.0192L395.22304 211.47648a36.864 36.864 0 1 0-52.14208 52.10112l259.6864 259.72736-259.76832 261.69344a36.864 36.864 0 0 0 52.30592 51.93728l285.696-287.744z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></div></div> <div data-v-9445e104="" data-v-56fb9f7a="" class="toolcall-content"><div data-v-9445e104="" class="resize-container" style="height: 0px; opacity: 0;"><div data-v-9445e104="" class="slot-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-56fb9f7a="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user is asking whether the annotation engine / skill primitives library they are building is significant enough for the robotics community. This is a subjective/strategic question, not a code question.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should give an honest, balanced assessment. The project as described so far is:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">An annotation engine that segments raw demonstration trajectories into labeled primitives (grasp, reach, etc.)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Generates natural language descriptions for those segments</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Has a composable primitive zoo/registry</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I need to evaluate:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Is this a real gap in the field?</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Is it technically novel enough?</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">What would make it significant vs. just another internal tool?</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Key context:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Open X-Embodiment dataset exists but is raw trajectory data without semantic skill segmentation at the primitive level</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">RT-1/RT-2, ACT, Diffusion Policy are end-to-end or task-level</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">There is growing interest in "skill libraries" and "primitive-based learning" (e.g., LOREL, SayCan, Code as Policies, etc.)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But there isn't a standard open-source annotation tool for segmenting manipulation trajectories into composable primitives with NL descriptions</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should be honest: the current scope (a few primitives + annotation script) is not yet significant on its own. But the <em>direction</em> is highly significant if executed properly. I should explain what would make it a real contribution:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Scale of dataset</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Diversity of primitives</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Quality of NL annotations</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Composability demonstrations</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Integration with real robot policies</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I should not overhype it, but I should validate that the problem they're solving is real and underserved.</div></div></div></div></div> </div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----><!----> <div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Not yet — but you're pointing at something that could be.</strong></div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Here's the honest breakdown:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>What you have right now</strong>
A clean annotation pipeline for segmenting demonstration trajectories into <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grasp</code> / <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">reach</code> / <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">retract</code> primitives with natural-language labels. That's good engineering, but in isolation it's an internal tool. The robotics community won't adopt a repo that just says "we can detect gripper closures."</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Why the <em>direction</em> is significant</strong>
The field has a real, unsolved problem here:</div><div data-v-01af594f="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="table markdown-table"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-01af594f="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-01af594f="" class="table-actions"><div data-v-01af594f="" class="table-actions-content"><span data-v-01af594f="" class="table-title">Table</span> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-01af594f=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span> <div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-01af594f="" class="icon-button table-actions-icon" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Download"><path d="M512 126.976a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 36.864 36.864v347.91424l119.11168-119.11168a36.864 36.864 0 1 1 52.14208 52.14208l-182.0672 182.02624a36.864 36.864 0 0 1-52.10112 0l-182.0672-182.02624a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 52.14208-52.14208l119.11168 119.11168V163.84a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 36.864-36.864zM184.32 564.71552a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 36.864 36.864v145.65376a35.96288 35.96288 0 0 0 35.96288 35.96288h509.70624a35.96288 35.96288 0 0 0 35.96288-35.96288v-145.65376a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 73.728 0v145.65376a109.69088 109.69088 0 0 1-109.69088 109.69088H257.14688A109.69088 109.69088 0 0 1 147.456 747.23328v-145.65376a36.864 36.864 0 0 1 36.864-36.864z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <!----></div><!----></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-01af594f="" class="table-container"><table data-v-01af594f=""><thead data-v-01af594f=""><tr data-v-01af594f=""><th data-v-01af594f="" align="left">What exists</th><th data-v-01af594f="" align="left">The gap you're targeting</th></tr></thead> <tbody data-v-01af594f=""><tr data-v-01af594f=""><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left"><strong data-v-01af594f="">Open X-Embodiment</strong> — massive raw trajectory datasets</td><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left">No standard semantic segmentation into reusable skill primitives</td></tr><tr data-v-01af594f=""><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left"><strong data-v-01af594f="">RT-1/RT-2, ACT, Diffusion Policy</strong> — end-to-end task learning</td><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left">No composability: "pick up cup" is one monolithic policy, not <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">approach → grasp → lift</code></td></tr><tr data-v-01af594f=""><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left"><strong data-v-01af594f="">SayCan, Code as Policies</strong> — high-level planning with LLMs</td><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left">No shared, validated primitive library underneath. Everyone reinvents "grasp"</td></tr><tr data-v-01af594f=""><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left"><strong data-v-01af594f="">LOREL, VLP</strong> — language-conditioned policies</td><td data-v-01af594f="" align="left">Language is tied to full tasks, not decomposed into primitive-level descriptions</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>What would make this a real contribution to robotics</strong></div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>A published dataset</strong><br>If you run your <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">annotate</code> engine over Open X-Embodiment (or your own collected data) and release <strong>trajectories + primitive segment labels + NL descriptions</strong> on Hugging Face, that becomes a citation-worthy asset. Right now, nobody has a standard "ManipulationNet" segmented at the primitive level.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>A validated primitive vocabulary</strong><br>Your <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">zoo</code> needs to grow from 3 primitives to 20–30, with clear taxonomy (<code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grasp</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">twist</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">slide</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">insert</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">pour</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">wipe</code>, etc.). If the community agrees "yes, these are the right atomic units," it becomes a standard.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Composability proof</strong><br>The transformer must show that <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">compose("approach", "grasp", "retract")</code> generalizes to unseen objects better than end-to-end imitation on the same data. That's the paper.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Real robot validation</strong><br>Annotation on simulation data is table stakes. Execution on a real Franka/UR5 with the composed primitives is what makes it robotics rather than signal processing.</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>The verdict</strong>
Your current repo is <strong>infrastructure for a significant idea</strong>. The idea — <em>a shared, annotated, composable primitive library for manipulation</em> — is absolutely significant. Consumer robotics desperately needs this: you can't ship a home robot that retrained a diffusion policy for every household task.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But the repo as it stands today is a build system and a base class. <strong>Ship the dataset, ship the composed policy results, and this becomes important.</strong> Keep building.</div></div></div> <!----> <!----> <!----></div>  <!----> <!----> <!----> <!---->   <!----> <div data-v-b1950b46="" class="okc-cards-container"><!----> <!----> <!----> <!----> <!----></div> <!----> <!----> <!----> <div data-v-b1bd13bb="" data-v-a660adc4="" class="segment-assistant-actions"><div data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="segment-assistant-actions-content"> <!----> <!----> <!----><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-b1bd13bb="" class="icon-button" style="width: 28px; height: 28px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <!----><