iv data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-56fb9f7a="" class="markdown-container toolcall-content-text"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The user wants the reach primitive (reach.py, spec.yaml, <strong>init</strong>.py) following the same pattern as grasp, lift, and place. Reach is the pre-grasp approach — moving toward an object with an open gripper. I should detect segments where the gripper is open and there's significant end-effector displacement toward the target (could use velocity magnitude or position change). Let me design it consistently with the others.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Detection logic for reach:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Gripper must be open (&gt;= OPEN_THRESHOLD) throughout</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Significant end-effector displacement (using velocity magnitude or position delta)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Could look at velocity norm if velocity is provided, or position deltas from state</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Precedes grasp, so it's the approach phase</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I'll use velocity if provided, otherwise fall back to position deltas from state. Actually to keep it simple and consistent, I'll use state position deltas (Euclidean distance) since state is commonly available. Or better: use velocity norm if provided, else compute from state differences.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me keep it clean: check gripper open, then check if there's sustained motion (using velocity norm or position deltas). I'll make it robust.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the pattern: grasp uses gripper transitions, lift uses Z rise with closed gripper, place uses open transition after closed. For reach, the signature is: gripper open + approaching motion (high velocity or decreasing distance to target).</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Since we don't have a target position in the raw trajectory, the simplest robust signature is: gripper open + significant end-effector velocity/motion. I'll use velocity norm if velocity is provided, otherwise compute speed from state position differences.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me write it with velocity norm as primary, falling back to state-derived speed.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Hyperparameters:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">OPEN_THRESHOLD = 0.5</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">MIN_SPEED_THRESHOLD = 0.01 (m/s or m per timestep)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">MIN_DURATION = 3</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">PRE_WINDOW = 3</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">POST_WINDOW = 2</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Confidence based on speed magnitude and duration.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me generate all three files.</div></div></div></div></div> </div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----><!----> <div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"></div></div><div data-v-004597db="" class="container-block"><div data-v-004597db="" class="block-item"><div data-v-d664c8c3=""><div data-v-863d6f1b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="toolcall-container default toolcall-ipython"><div data-v-bc9c95fe="" data-v-863d6f1b="" class="toolcall-title-container default"><div data-v-bc9c95fe="" class="toolcall-title-name line"><svg data-v-bc9c95fe="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify toolcall-title-name-icon" width="1em" height="1em" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Code"><path d="M705.28 642.56a35.541333 35.541333 0 0 1 0 70.741333l-3.669333 0.170667H512a35.541333 35.541333 0 0 1 0-71.082667h189.610667l3.626666 0.170667zM297.216 320.938667a35.584 35.584 0 0 1 47.573333-2.432l2.730667 2.432 114.261333 114.261333a75.093333 75.093333 0 0