ve the same issue:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">grasp/__init__.py</code>: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from grasp.grasp import Grasp</code> → should be <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from skill_primitives.primitives.grasp.grasp import Grasp</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">lift/__init__.py</code>: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from lift.lift import Lift</code> → should be <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from skill_primitives.primitives.lift.lift import Lift</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">etc.</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But wait - the user only showed the error for <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">base</code>. Let me check if the other ones would also fail. Yes, they all use the same pattern. I should fix all of them.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the traceback more carefully:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives/__init__.py</code> imports from <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives.primitives.base</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives/primitives/__init__.py</code> imports from <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives.primitives.base</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives/primitives/base/__init__.py</code> does <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from base.base import Primitive</code></div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The issue is that <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">base</code> is not a top-level package. When Python tries to resolve <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from base.base import Primitive</code>, it looks for a top-level module named <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">base</code>, which doesn't exist. The correct absolute import is <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from skill_primitives.primitives.base.base import Primitive</code>.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Same pattern applies to all primitive subpackages. Let me regenerate all the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">__init__.py</code> files with absolute imports.</div></div></div></div></div> </div></div></div></div> <!----> <!----><!----> <div data-v-3b9ae98b="" data-v-d664c8c3="" class="markdown-container"><div data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="markdown"><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The error is in the primitive <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">__init__.py</code> files — they use bare relative-style imports (<code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from base.base import Primitive</code>) that Python resolves as top-level modules. They need to be absolute imports under the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives</code> namespace.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Here are all the fixed <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">__init__.py</code> files:</div></div></div><div data-v-004597db="" class="container-block"><div data-v-004597db="" class="block-item"><div data-v-d664c8c3=""><div data-