</strong>:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">classifiers</code> include 3.9, 3.10, 3.11, 3.12</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[tool.black]</code> has <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py311"]</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[tool.ruff]</code> has <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = "py39"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">[tool.mypy]</code> has <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">python_version = "3.10"</code></div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This is inconsistent. The CI was running Python 3.9 and 3.10, but the devcontainer is 3.11. The black target is py311, but ruff target is py39, and mypy is 3.10.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Black formatting issues</strong>: The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">extend-exclude</code> uses triple-quoted string with newlines, which black might reformat. Actually, looking at the CI error, black was trying to parse for py312 but the pyproject says py311. The issue was that some files had syntax that black couldn't parse.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Ruff lint issues</strong>: The ruff config looks mostly fine, but I should make sure <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">known-first-party</code> is correct.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Coverage duplicate</strong>: There's a duplicate <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline"># ── Coverage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</code> comment.</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">extend-exclude</code> in black</strong>: This uses a raw string with newlines inside triple quotes. Black itself formats TOML, but this is about the pyproject.toml file being valid. The issue is more about the content being correct.</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me think about what needs to change:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Align Python versions</strong>: Since the devcontainer is 3.11 and the black target is py311, I should align everything to 3.11 minimum, or keep 3.9 support but make the tools consistent. Looking at the user's code, they use <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">|</code> union syntax (<code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">dict | None</code>) which requires <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code> on Python &lt; 3.10, but they have that. However, the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">ClassVar</code> and other modern typing works with <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code> on 3.9+.</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But wait - the CI was failing on Python 3.9 and 3.10 because of syntax errors in files (unterminated strings), not because of Python version incompatibility. The actual code we generated uses <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code> and <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">X | None</code> syntax which is fine on 3.9+ with that import.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">However, the user said the devcontainer is 3.11 and they want green ticks. Let me look at what would make everything consistent:</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Option A: Support 3.9+</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = "py39"</code> for ruff</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py39"]</code> for black</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">python_version = "3.9"</code> for mypy</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Option B: Support 3.11+</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.11"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = "py311"</code> for ruff</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py311"]</code> for black</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">python_version = "3.11"</code> for mypy</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Given the devcontainer uses 3.11 and the user seems to be developing there, but they also had CI running 3.9 and 3.10... The safest is to keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">&gt;=3.9</code> support but align all tools to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">py39</code> minimum. However, the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">|</code> union syntax without <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__</code> would fail on 3.9. But we always include <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code>, so that's fine.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the current pyproject.toml:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">black: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py311"]</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">ruff: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = "py39"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">mypy: <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">python_version = "3.10"</code></div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The black target being py311 while ruff is py39 is the main inconsistency. The CI error said "Python 3.11 cannot parse code formatted for Python 3.12" - wait, that was because black was running on Python 3.11 but trying to parse code that might have 3.12 syntax? No, the error was "cannot format ... for target version Python 3.12" which means black thought the target was py312. But the pyproject says py311. That suggests the CI was using an older black version or there was a mismatch.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, re-reading the CI error:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">plain</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89.514667 89.514667 0 0 0 554.666667 209.066667H298.666667A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 209.066667 298.666667v256c0 33.109333 17.92 62.08 44.8 77.653333a38.4 38.4 0 0 1-38.4 66.474667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 132.266667 554.666667V298.666667A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 298.666667 132.266667h256z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg> <div data-v-5f9e9bf6="" data-v-1005b780="" class="kimi-tooltip tooltip-bottom icon-button-tooltip bottom">Copy</div></div></span></div></header></div></div></div> <div data-v-efb858b9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="syntax-highlighter dark segment-code-content"><pre data-v-efb858b9="" class="language-plain"><code class="language-plain">Warning: Python 3.11 cannot parse code formatted for Python 3.12. To fix this: run Black with Python 3.12, set --target-version to py311, or use --fast to skip the safety check.
error: cannot format ...: Cannot parse for target version Python 3.12</code></pre></div></div> <div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This is weird because the pyproject.toml says <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py311"]</code>. Maybe the CI was using a different black config or the pyproject.toml wasn't being read properly. Or maybe the files had syntax that was invalid even for py311 parsing.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, looking more carefully: "Cannot parse for target version Python 3.12" - this suggests black defaulted to py312 because it was running on Python 3.12? But the env shows Python 3.11.15. Hmm, maybe the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version</code> wasn't being picked up because of how black was invoked.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, I think the issue is simpler: the files had syntax errors (unterminated strings), and black's parser (which runs before formatting) failed. The error message about "target version Python 3.12" is a red herring or indicates black was trying to parse with the host Python version.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me focus on what the user actually needs:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">A clean, consistent pyproject.toml</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">All tool versions aligned</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">No formatting issues in the TOML itself (though TOML isn't typically formatted by black, it should be valid)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Proper exclusions so build_zoo.py and generated files don't get linted unnecessarily</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Key fixes:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><strong>Align all tool targets to py39</strong> (since requires-python is &gt;=3.9) OR <strong>py311</strong> (since devcontainer is 3.11). I'll go with py311 for the devcontainer alignment, but keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"</code> for broader compatibility. Actually, if we use py311 target, black might generate code that uses 3.11+ syntax (like <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">X | None</code> without <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__</code>), which would break on 3.9. So I should keep target at py39 or py310 to be safe, OR bump requires-python to &gt;=3.10 or &gt;=3.11.</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Looking at the user's code, they use <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">X | None</code> syntax in type annotations. With <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">from __future__ import annotations</code>, this is fine at runtime on 3.9+, but static analyzers and black need to know the target. Black's <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py311"]</code> means it might use 3.11-specific formatting features, but more importantly, it parses the code assuming 3.11 grammar.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">I think the best approach is:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Keep <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">requires-python = "&gt;=3.9"</code> for install compatibility</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Set black target to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">["py39"]</code> to ensure code works on 3.9+</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Set ruff target to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">"py39"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Set mypy to <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">"3.9"</code></div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">This ensures consistency</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">But wait, the user is using <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">numpy</code> type hints like <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">np.ndarray</code> which might need newer numpy for good typing. And <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">ClassVar</code> from typing is fine on 3.9.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the CI, it was running Python 3.9.25 and 3.10.20 and 3.11.15 in different jobs. The tests were failing on 3.9 due to import errors (from the <strong>init</strong>.py relative imports), not syntax. So 3.9 compatibility is important.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me align everything to py39:</div><ul start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = ["py39"]</code> for black</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">target-version = "py39"</code> for ruff (already correct)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto"><code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">python_version = "3.9"</code> for mypy</div></li></ul><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Other issues to fix:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The duplicate <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline"># ── Coverage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</code> line</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">extend-exclude</code> in black uses triple quotes with newlines - this is valid TOML but let's make sure it's clean</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Add <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">zoo/</code> and <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">script/</code> to ruff exclusions if needed</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Make sure <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">known-first-party</code> includes <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">skill_primitives</code></div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Wait, the user said "make it black formatted and ruff linted to be green tick". The pyproject.toml itself doesn't get formatted by black (black formats Python code), but the TOML should be valid. Ruff doesn't lint TOML. So the user probably means: update the pyproject.toml configuration so that when black and ruff run on the Python files, they pass.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">The key config changes:</div><ol start="1"><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Align black target-version with the minimum supported Python (py39)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Fix the TOML structure to be valid and clean</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Ensure exclusions cover generated files (zoo/, etc.)</div></li><li><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Remove the duplicate coverage header comment</div></li></ol><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me also check: the user had issues with <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">build_zoo.py</code> being reformatted by black. The current pyproject has <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">line-length = 100</code> for both black and ruff, which is good.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">One more thing: the <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">extend-exclude</code> in black uses <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">'''</code> with a regex pattern. This is fine but let's make sure it's properly formatted.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Actually, looking at the CI error again, black was complaining about files with syntax errors (unterminated strings). Those files (<code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">exporters.py</code>, <code data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code-inline">compose.py</code>, etc.) are NOT in the pyproject.toml exclusions. We should probably not exclude them - they need to be fixed. But the user said "All these files are green tick ATM" meaning they're currently passing. So maybe those syntax errors were from an older run.</div><div class="paragraph" dir="auto">Let me focus on generating a clean, consistent pyproject.toml:</div><div data-v-8d822fd1="" data-v-3b9ae98b="" class="segment-code"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" data-v-8d822fd1="" class="sticky-release" style="--sticky-header-height: 42.5px;"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-rail"><div data-v-13fde6a9="" class="sticky-release-header" style="top: 0px;"><header data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header"><div data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-header-content"><span data-v-8d822fd1="" class="segment-code-lang">toml</span> <!----> <span data-v-868ea423="" data-v-8d822fd1=""><div data-v-1005b780="" data-v-868ea423="" class="icon-button" style="width: 32px; height: 32px;"><svg data-v-1005b780="" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" role="img" class="iconify" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 1024 1024" name="Copy"><path d="M725.333333 302.933333a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 166.4 166.4v256a166.4 166.4 0 0 1-166.4 166.4h-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 302.933333 725.333333v-256A166.4 166.4 0 0 1 469.333333 302.933333h256z m-256 76.8A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 379.733333 469.333333v256c0 49.493333 40.106667 89.6 89.6 89.6h256a89.6 89.6 0 0 0 89.6-89.6v-256A89.6 89.6 0 0 0 725.333333 379.733333h-256z" fill="currentColor"></path><path d="M554.666667 132.266667a166.4 166.4 0 0 1 144.128 83.2 38.4 38.4 0 0 1-66.517334 38.4A89